Feature: Web application firewall on a route
  A route on the public Gateway opts into a web application firewall, Coraza with the OWASP Core Rule Set, run by the Gateway's own proxies as a Wasm filter enabled on that route alone, by taking one component, as it takes sign-in. The demo route hello takes it in blocking mode, so an obvious injection in its query string is refused while a plain request and the Gateway check's own request pass. No other route's requests go through the filter, and no object a file under kubernetes/ writes or the render holds puts the firewall on the Gateway as a whole or on a route that did not opt in. Outside what these scenarios read are a mutation or a generation by an admission controller at run time, the roles that may write such a policy, the objects a Flux Kustomization applies from a source the render does not follow, and what another tool applies to the cluster from a file outside kubernetes/, such as a manifest inline in a Talos patch.

  Scenario: A route opts into the web application firewall with one component, and no policy puts it on the Gateway as a whole
    Given the component waf, every file under kubernetes/ and every application the lab-sto2 overlay enables, built as Flux applies them, with what the charts of their HelmReleases render
    When the component's objects and variables, every EnvoyExtensionPolicy, every object with a Wasm extension, every EnvoyProxy and EnvoyPatchPolicy, every object that names either policy kind or has a Wasm key, every List and every Flux object, and the demo routes hello and hello-sso are read
    Then the component is one EnvoyExtensionPolicy named after the route, whose targetRefs name that one HTTPRoute of the Gateway API group and nothing else, with no target selector, merge type or reference to another namespace, from the two variables WAF_ROUTE and WAF_MODE alone, with no default, which its header documents; every EnvoyExtensionPolicy under kubernetes/ and of what the charts render is the component's, one for each Kustomization that takes it, and targets only HTTPRoutes of its own namespace that the render holds; no object targets a Gateway or a ListenerSet with a Wasm extension; no EnvoyProxy registers a Wasm module and no EnvoyPatchPolicy exists, in a file under kubernetes/ or a chart's render; the render holds no List with items and no Flux object a chart renders, and no object of it names either kind or its resource, or has a key Wasm modules are given under, but the component's policies, the Kustomizations that take it and the controller's own ClusterRole; the routes behind the firewall are demo/hello alone; and hello-sso, on the same backend, has no policy

  Scenario: The firewall is Coraza with the Core Rule Set at its defaults, from an image pinned by tag and the digest the controller checks, and it fails open
    Given the demo route hello as the lab-sto2 overlay applies it, every application the overlay enables, and the public registry of the filter's image
    When the route's policy, its one Wasm entry and the image's index and manifest are read
    Then the entry is coraza from an Image source whose URL is the filter's image by tag and digest with no checksum of its own, fails open, and is configured with the recommended configuration, the route's rule engine mode, the Core Rule Set's setup and every rule file of it and nothing else; no policy adds a rule, an action, a paranoia level, a threshold, an exclusion or an audit setting; the digest is of an image manifest with one gzipped tar layer, which the tag's index lists for linux/amd64, and not of the index; and the Renovate comment above the URL names the image

  Scenario: The demo route's firewall blocks, a route may choose detection only, and a route's health shows whether its firewall was accepted
    Given every ks.yaml of the tree that takes the component, the demo route hello as the lab-sto2 overlay applies it, the same route built with its mode set to DetectionOnly, and statuses of the policy's shape
    When their substitutions, health checks, waits and rendered directives are read, and the health expressions are evaluated against those statuses
    Then hello takes the component with WAF_ROUTE hello and WAF_MODE On and still waits on gateway-public, hello and blackbox-exporter alone; every ks.yaml that takes the component sets WAF_ROUTE and WAF_MODE On or DetectionOnly, waits, and holds the policy's acceptance as its health, current while every ancestor accepts the policy at its generation, failed once one refuses it at its generation, and neither without an ancestor or at an earlier generation, with the expressions the component's header gives; and the rendered rule engine line is the mode's, On for hello and DetectionOnly when the route chooses it

  Scenario: The proxies fetch the firewall's module from the controller, and the controller alone reaches the registry, on 443
    Given the render of the tree, with its real charts
    When the policies of envoy-gateway-system are evaluated for the flows of the module, the controller's Service and pod are read, and the policy that lets the controller out is read
    Then the proxies reach the controller on 18002, the port the controller's Service names wasm and its pod serves the cached modules on, where no other application's pod and nothing outside the cluster reaches it; the controller reaches the world on 443 and not on 80, by a policy of its own, envoy-gateway-registry, whose one rule is that flow and which says why; and neither the certificate job nor the proxies reach the world
